## The aggregate, which is stronger than any single cell

**28 of 28 R1 cells came in below 1.0**, across 6 presets x 3 sizes x 2 qps.
The control arm — a binary byte-identical to its own base — was 13 below and 15
above. A sign test on 28/28 is p = 3.7e-9. The five cells that fall inside the
band individually are still part of that run of 28; they are reported as null
cell-by-cell and are not counted as evidence on their own.

The effect size also tracks the census
(`benchmarks/txunit_census_2026-08-13.tsv`), which put the discarded share of
inverse-transform PIXEL work at 40-50 % at p10/p13, 36-50 % at p8, 43-51 % at
p7, 28-53 % at p6 and 24-44 % at p2 — largest at the fast presets, where the
measured speedup is also largest.

## Byte-identity

byteid_fingerprint 168/168, `port_md5 == c_md5` on all 168, whole file diffs
CLEAN against the pre-session fingerprint. decode_gate_grid 120/120 (aomdec +
dav1d, byte-identical to C). partial_sb_gate 146/146. recon_parity 432/432 —
and that gate is not vacuous here: 199 of 432 streams fired CDEF over 2,461,408
filtered pixels and 118 signalled wiener, unchanged from the base run.
nextest --workspace 1029/1029.

## Not done

* The bd10 mirror `tx_unit_hbd` still reconstructs unconditionally. Same
  defect, same three sites, untouched — it is reachable only from the bd10
  re-encode pass, which no gap cell runs.
* `TxUnitOut::qcoeff` is also unread at all three sites (`compute_cul_level`'s
  `cul` likewise). Removing the qcoeff ESCAPE is R4's pooling question, not
  R1's, and `qcoeff` is still an input to `cost_coeffs_txb` wherever the exact
  rate survives — so it cannot simply be dropped.
